[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-47367"] :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ 2, & x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is **not** continuous at $x=1$ but it is differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is **not** differentiable on the interval $x\\in (-2,2)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement individually.\n\n- Statement I is false. First, we investigate the continuity of $f(x)$ . It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}{x}^{2}={1}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it is a removable discontinuity).\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1,$ it is also not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval that includes this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable in $x\\in (-2,2).$\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-73249/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "236bd77a-fe48-50f8-8aba-49c8e6cbfa7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73249/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "0dc4e6e1-b458-57e6-a617-8bb50b8686cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73249/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "a565db49-640b-5346-bd78-c62d22fa9fd0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73249/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "f0132a0a-2d48-5474-8ff0-2006be8cd30b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73249/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "f647ee41-39b8-56e5-9de1-a37ee0ea1d03" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73249/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0e52457a-2386-5a3b-a371-cc79ad18a5eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-73249/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-73249/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-73249/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-73249/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-73249/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-73249/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-73249" :question/id #uuid "9426dbc5-fee7-5af9-83b3-97f66b9b1d25" :question/math-academy-id "q-73249" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996853/assets/ec8101640f2bd84506d1c47b92f088675080d87eafff4000a4f3e0d14ccebb55.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=-2$\n- The function is continuous at $x=1$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is false. From the graph we can see that $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking the pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement III is false. The graph of $f(x)$ shows a cusp at this point and so it is not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-73249/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "794398c0-ffbf-5971-a411-f236607f56b8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-73249"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-73260/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "1f4f6ed6-8a29-5f9b-b2ef-ae62fe125854" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73260/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "bb2f1210-8ec9-5abc-967f-f8c08c42095c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73260/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "fd1aa557-95f0-5485-b2b2-3bfe1c84f2e4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73260/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "35e89a85-3627-59f7-b95e-3e90d18f30ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73260/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "0dcc79b2-5ee4-563c-a5e1-e07f28af2bab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73260/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "eaf00d60-8c08-5d22-9496-a7172d67adad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-73260/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-73260/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-73260/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-73260/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-73260/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-73260/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-73260" :question/id #uuid "de43e357-7e2a-5e20-ae7c-f5be72dd85e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-73260" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a corner point at $x=2,$ and is differentiable at $x=4$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "- Statement I is true. Differentiability implies continuity. Hence, ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a corner point at $x=2$, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- Statement III is false.  Continuity does not imply differentiability. For instance, the function  $g(x)=|x|$ is continuous at $x=0$ but it is not differentiable there." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-73260/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-73260"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-73264/selection/0f736474153de82c50d8a50694b55b330d4b7dd82b8d62afd0cce8dd02d8e913" :answer/id #uuid "c7bbc16d-af0b-5881-b70b-ad8052c62453" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73264/selection/0eb9518c3a0a32028ed2a84cadde9fc0bdef88de01cac490e1c76e6f4a114ae3" :answer/id #uuid "4dd4f175-0e2d-52a0-bd1d-cec2f642f503" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73264/selection/2091d141347210e8365490488f43298975ccec5af869e0828395972a5e38d152" :answer/id #uuid "3cf073db-21c6-5479-8835-b1bc8ee8422d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73264/selection/ce36152bba16fea3799bb96150c5421e96ede11bc41f90848b21cd6cdf4d92c2" :answer/id #uuid "91b223e8-060c-5caf-bf71-5a173edd4138" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & x<1 \\\\ x+2,\\, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73264/selection/20a79d4bc5b9d378de4012822f266dc0c90fe8968a9394a8d7329c41e0b122bf" :answer/id #uuid "7ddb2ced-7fc7-5bff-b3de-7d7b9ce84f77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "h(x)=2|x-1|" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73264/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8809e040-3a4f-5b40-93ee-ddba22f73a0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-73264/selection/2091d141347210e8365490488f43298975ccec5af869e0828395972a5e38d152" :answer-field/choices ["q-73264/selection/0f736474153de82c50d8a50694b55b330d4b7dd82b8d62afd0cce8dd02d8e913" "q-73264/selection/0eb9518c3a0a32028ed2a84cadde9fc0bdef88de01cac490e1c76e6f4a114ae3" "q-73264/selection/2091d141347210e8365490488f43298975ccec5af869e0828395972a5e38d152" "q-73264/selection/ce36152bba16fea3799bb96150c5421e96ede11bc41f90848b21cd6cdf4d92c2" "q-73264/selection/20a79d4bc5b9d378de4012822f266dc0c90fe8968a9394a8d7329c41e0b122bf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-73264" :question/id #uuid "3e1e5ddc-8e90-5bdf-a10e-7ce2c1147053" :question/math-academy-id "q-73264" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the function $p(x)$ is differentiable for all $x>-1.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $p(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}.$\n\nThe other functions are not differentiable at $x=1.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ has a corner point at $x=1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=1$, and hence it is not differentiable there." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-73264/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b4100f8-8a66-5243-b0b6-c36b9d17dac3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-73264"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-101997/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "9608d249-ff60-5ec9-afa3-323309b776f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101997/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "7ad87844-6c35-5685-9feb-63269ad45b2b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101997/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "43c57746-d217-5cd3-b82a-f65897436695" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101997/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "63e99eb8-84f1-5ef8-be7b-e964a4e34726" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101997/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "1e430fd9-fc23-5456-83e1-9c093a8e5bc8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-101997/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "802981d9-5f74-590b-b09c-15247a914e2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-101997/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-101997/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-101997/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-101997/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-101997/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-101997/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-101997" :question/id #uuid "c0c690a4-aac6-53e2-93ef-bbe0c027b941" :question/math-academy-id "q-101997" :question/problem "Given that a function $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-5$, is differentiable at $x=-2$, and has a  vertical tangent at $x=0$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=-5$\n- It  is continuous at $x=-2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiability implies continuity. Hence,\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-5$, it can't be differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is true.   Since $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=-2$ it is also  continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since we have a vertical tangent at $x=0$, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at this point." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-101997/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7dcbd56f-0fc2-59c5-a016-4272be4734c7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-101997"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
